column2:
[ ... I ba]rred my door,
27	[ ... I went up t]o the roof;
28	[ ... ] let me learn [your ro]ad(?).

[S]o I said: I will go and see Ūta-napišti the Far-away, of whom they keep telling.
251	I turned; I traveled through all the lands, every one;
252	I crossed and crossed again the punishing mountains;
253	I crossed and crossed again the seas, every one.
254	Of sweet sleep my face got no fill;
255	I ground myself down with sleeplessness;
256	I filled my sinews with grieving.
257	And [w]hat have I gathered by my toi[l?]
258	I had not reached the ale-wife's house when my clothing was done;
259	[I kill]ed bear, hyena, lion, leopard, tiger-cat,
260	deer, ibex — the herds and beasts of the open land;
261	I eat their flesh; I fla[y] their skins for cover.
262	As for grief — let them bar her door;
263	with pitch and bitumen let them se[al her door(?) shut!]
264	On my account there is no [ ... ] of play;
265	on my account, glad-and-grieving, [ ... ]
